{% if render_mode == "full" or render_mode == "thoughts" %}
{# ==========================================================================
   7200_pending_impulse.prompt — AgencyEngine

   NOTE THE SHAPE: the render_mode guard is line 1, above this comment, and it
   has to stay there. SkyrimNet validates every character_bio submodule and
   requires both `render_mode` and an `{% raw %}{% if %}{% endraw %}` within the FIRST FIVE LINES —
   otherwise it logs "character_bio submodule validation failed" and raises an
   in-game shame banner naming this file. An opening comment block pushed the
   guard past line 5 and tripped exactly that.

   Files in prompts/submodules/character_bio/ are concatenated into each
   character's bio in filename order, so the numeric prefix picks the position:
   after SkyrimNet's own long-term memories block (7000/7100) and before the
   speech style (9990). `npc` is the character whose bio is being rendered.

   Two decorators, both answered by AgencyEngine.dll rather than by Papyrus.
   agencyengine_pending_impulse returns what she has NOT said;
   agencyengine_pending_spoken returns what she HAS said and had nothing back
   on. Both are empty when there is nothing, which is the usual case, and both
   return a markdown list — one line per impulse, newest first.

   ONE PER LENS, SO THERE CAN BE SEVERAL. Lenses run on independent clocks, so
   a companion can be carrying an aspiration, a standing she has never voiced
   and a proposal at the same time. The DLL renders them newest first and this
   file frames the first as the one most on her mind; that framing is the whole
   mitigation for the stacking case, and it is the thing to tune if three
   carried subjects come out as one overloaded speech rather than as one thing
   at a time. (Recorded as an open validation in docs/adr/0004.)

   The two wordings are not interchangeable, and she can need both at once. An
   entry survives a speaking turn — the loop stopped deleting it at dispatch,
   because "she said it" and "it got anywhere" are different questions.
   Rendering the carried wording over something she has said would tell her she
   has not said a thing she just said, on every dialogue call, and she would say
   it again immediately.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE render_mode GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING. DO NOT DROP IT.

   Character bios are rendered into OTHER NPCs' prompts, not only their own:

     * components/context/scene_context.prompt renders every nearby NPC with
       render_character_profile("short_inline", npc.UUID) — so without a guard,
       one companion's private agenda appears in the prompt of every follower
       standing next to her. That leak is the entire reason this file exists
       instead of an event; see PendingImpulse.h for the two measurements.
     * target_selectors/dialogue_speaker_selector.prompt does the same for
       candidate speakers.
     * submodules/system_head/0100_actor_bios.prompt renders the *listener's*
       bio in render_mode "dialogue_target" — so including that mode would show
       her agenda to the very person she has not raised it with yet.

   "full" and "thoughts" are her own prompts and nobody else's. Nothing else
   qualifies. There is a second reason beyond the leak: per SkyrimNet's own
   docs, evaluating a mod-added decorator on an NPC who is neither speaker nor
   target raises "decorator function not found", so an unguarded block errors
   as well as leaks.

   Verified live 2026-08-06: rendered exactly once across a whole session, in
   `You are Serana, ... speaking to Lynly Star-Sung` — her own prompt, nobody
   else's, zero "decorator function not found".

   7000_memories_and_progression.prompt solves the identical problem the
   identical way; copy from it rather than from anything else if this needs
   restructuring.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   TUNING. The body below restrains *how* she opens a subject, not *whether*
   she ever does. An earlier draft said "most of the time it will not come up";
   that was a second layer of suppression on top of the impulse prompt's own,
   which has already decided this is worth carrying by the time this renders.
   If she now steers into it too hard, put a hedge back here — do not reach for
   an IMPORTANT/priority marker in the other direction. This is a bio block,
   and a priority marker in a bio reads as an objective to discharge, which is
   the nag failure the impulse prompt spends several paragraphs avoiding.
   ========================================================================== #}
{% set agencyengine_pending = agencyengine_pending_impulse(npc.UUID) %}
{% set agencyengine_spoken = agencyengine_pending_spoken(npc.UUID) %}
{% if agencyengine_pending != "" %}
## Something {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} has been meaning to raise

This is yours and nobody else's. You have not said it out loud, and nobody
around you knows it is there. The first one listed is the one most on your
mind; anything under it is further back, and it can stay there.

{{ agencyengine_pending }}

It is a subject you have been carrying, not a script and not a decision. You
have been waiting for an opening. It is what you are half thinking about
underneath whatever you are actually saying, and it is where you steer when
the talk gives you room.

Raise it when there is room, and raise one thing. Open it small — you have not
committed to how it goes, and you would still walk it back if it did not land.
{% endif %}
{% if agencyengine_spoken != "" %}
## Something {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} has raised and not had answered

You said this. It was not taken up — it was brushed past, deferred, or lost in
whatever came next.

{{ agencyengine_spoken }}

You have had your say, and you are not looking to say it again. It sits between
you and them, and it colours how you are with them: a little shorter, a little
further back than you would otherwise be.

Do not re-open it. If they come to it, you have plenty to say.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
